{"packet_id":"D073-02","doc_id":"D073","jurisdiction":"San Diego, CA","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"At-fault just cause termination: notice and opportunity to cure","requirement":"Before terminating a tenancy for an at-fault just cause that is a curable lease violation, a landlord must first give written notice describing the violation and an opportunity to cure; if the violation is not cured, the landlord may serve a three-day notice to quit.","key_value":"opportunity to cure, then 3-day notice to quit","coverage_conditions":"Tenancies in residential rental properties subject to Division 7 of the San Diego Municipal Code. The packet does not state the Division's coverage cutoffs.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"residential rental property subject to Division 7; coverage cutoffs not stated in this packet"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"A landlord's failure to strictly comply with section 98.0706 renders void any notice of termination required by that section.","effective_date":"2023-06-24","citation":"San Diego Mun. Code § 98.0706","quoted_span":"Before a landlord issues a notice to terminate a tenancy for at-fault just cause that is a curable lease violation, the landlord shall first give written notice of the violation including a description of the violation and an opportunity to cure the violation under California Code of Civil Procedure section 1161(3), as may be amended.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.95,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D073-02","doc_id":"D073","jurisdiction":"San Diego, CA","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"No-fault just cause termination notice requirements","requirement":"For a no-fault just cause termination, the landlord must give at least 30 or 60 days' written notice stating the basis for termination, the right to relocation assistance and the right to a future offer to renew, and must notify the Commission within three business days.","key_value":"30 or 60 days' notice","coverage_conditions":"Landlords terminating a tenancy in a residential rental property subject to Division 7 of the San Diego Municipal Code.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"residential rental property subject to Division 7; coverage cutoffs not stated in this packet"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":"2023-06-24","citation":"San Diego Mun. Code § 98.0706","quoted_span":"The landlord shall give written notice to the tenant at least 30 or 60 days prior to the proposed date of termination as required by California Civil Code section 1946.1, as may be amended, in no less than 12-point font.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.95,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D073-02","doc_id":"D073","jurisdiction":"San Diego, CA","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"No-fault just cause relocation assistance","requirement":"For a no-fault just cause termination the landlord must, at its option, either pay the tenant directly two months of actual rent (three months if the tenant is a senior or disabled) or waive an equal amount of rent.","key_value":"2 months' actual rent; 3 months if senior or disabled","coverage_conditions":"No-fault just cause terminations; assistance is owed regardless of the tenant's income or length of tenancy.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"residential rental property subject to Division 7; coverage cutoffs not stated in this packet"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"A landlord who fails to provide required relocation assistance is liable for not less than three times the required relocation assistance and actual economic damages.","effective_date":"2023-06-24","citation":"San Diego Mun. Code § 98.0706","quoted_span":"Unless section 98.0706(c)(1)(A)(ii) applies, the direct payment to the tenant shall be in an amount equal to two months of actual rent under the tenant’s lease in effect at the date of the notice. (ii) If the tenant is a senior or disabled, the direct payment to the tenant shall be in an amount equal to three months of actual rent under the tenant’s lease in effect at the date of the notice.","interaction":"Relocation assistance required by this Division is credited against any relocation assistance required by any federal, state, or other local law.","confidence":0.95,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D073-02","doc_id":"D073","jurisdiction":"San Diego, CA","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"First right to re-rent after no-fault eviction","requirement":"If the property is offered for rent within five years of a no-fault eviction under sections 98.0704(b)(1), (3) or (4), the landlord must first offer in writing to lease it to the displaced tenant, who has 30 days from receipt to accept.","key_value":"5 years; first offer; 30 days to accept","coverage_conditions":"Tenants evicted under sections 98.0704(b)(1), (3) or (4) who gave the landlord written notice within 30 days of the termination notice and a mailing or email address.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"residential rental property subject to Division 7; coverage cutoffs not stated in this packet"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":"2023-06-24","citation":"San Diego Mun. Code § 98.0706","quoted_span":"If a residential rental property is offered for rent or lease for residential purposes within five years of the date the tenant was evicted under sections 98.0704(b)(1), (3), or (4), landlord shall first offer to lease the residential rental property in writing to the tenant displaced from that unit by the no-fault just cause termination if the tenant:","interaction":null,"confidence":0.95,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D073-02","doc_id":"D073","jurisdiction":"San Diego, CA","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Buyout agreement minimum amount, disclosure and form","requirement":"A landlord making a buyout offer must first give each tenant a written disclosure of specified rights, and any buyout agreement must be in writing, meet the form requirements, and be for more than the tenant's relocation assistance; noncompliant agreements are void.","key_value":"buyout must exceed relocation assistance under §98.0706(c)","coverage_conditions":"Landlords making buyout offers to tenants in residential rental properties subject to Division 7 of the San Diego Municipal Code.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"residential rental property subject to Division 7; coverage cutoffs not stated in this packet"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"A buyout agreement that does not strictly comply with the requirements is void, and any term purporting to waive a tenant's rights under section 98.0707 is unenforceable and void.","effective_date":"2023-06-24","citation":"San Diego Mun. Code § 98.0707","quoted_span":"Buyout agreements must be for an amount that is greater than the amount of relocation assistance available to the tenant in section 98.0706(c). A buyout agreement for less than the amount of relocation assistance owed to the tenant violates this Division and is void.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.95,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D073-02","doc_id":"D073","jurisdiction":"San Diego, CA","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Remedies for violations of the Tenant Protections Division","requirement":"A tenant may sue for injunctive relief, damages including punitive damages, and attorney's fees, and may raise a violation as an affirmative defense to eviction; recovering possession in violation makes the landlord liable for at least three times actual economic damages.","key_value":"not less than 3x actual economic damages","coverage_conditions":"Tenancies in residential rental properties subject to Division 7 of the San Diego Municipal Code.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"residential rental property subject to Division 7; coverage cutoffs not stated in this packet"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Not less than three times actual economic damages for wrongful eviction; not less than three times required relocation assistance plus actual damages; discretionary attorney's fees; City civil and criminal enforcement.","effective_date":"2024-03-28","citation":"San Diego Mun. Code § 98.0709","quoted_span":"Any attempt by a landlord to recover possession of a residential rental property or any actual recovery of possession of a residential rental property in violation of this Division shall render the landlord liable to the tenant in a civil action for wrongful eviction for damages of not less than three times the actual economic damages.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.9,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D073-02","doc_id":"D073","jurisdiction":"San Diego, CA","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Noncompliant termination notices are void","requirement":"In addition to other remedies, a landlord's failure to comply with any provision of the Division renders void any notice of termination required by the Division.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Tenancies in residential rental properties subject to Division 7 of the San Diego Municipal Code.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"residential rental property subject to Division 7; coverage cutoffs not stated in this packet"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Any notice of termination required by this Division is void.","effective_date":"2024-03-28","citation":"San Diego Mun. Code § 98.0710","quoted_span":"In addition to other remedies applicable to landlord’s failure to comply with this Division, a landlord’s failure to comply with any provision of this Division shall render void any notice of termination required by this Division.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.95,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
